CHECKPOINT 2 — item 1's remainder and item 2's honest form are in.

WIDENED DERIVATION (tests/test_graph_files_are_read_under_one_policy.py::TestTheSkipPolicyLivesInOneBody). A reader is now 'a body that LISTS a directory (glob/rglob/iterdir/listdir/scandir/walk) and PARSES YAML (any PyYAML loader)' rather than 'a body that globs the literal *.yml and calls safe_load'. Five new cases, one per evasion route, over synthetic sources: iterdir, rglob, a glob pattern held in a variable, os.listdir, yaml.load with a Loader. VERIFIED each one FAILS against .24's narrow detector (measured by running the old detector over the new constants: all five return [] where the case expects the body). Two more cases hold the widening's own error direction — a body with only one half must NOT be named — and those two CANNOT fail against the pre-.25 tree, because the narrow detector also refuses them; what they fail against is a detector widened to 'either half', which is the mutation the widening invites. Declared as such. The wide detector finds exactly the same one body on this tree, so no exemption is needed today and the trade-off (under-report ships the defect, over-report fails in front of the author) is stated in the class docstring.

THE BRANCH AXIS (new class TestWhatEveryBranchOfInitDoesWithAFileItCannotHandle, same module). The axis is THE_TABLE imported from .19's module — 8 cells, entry point x mode, derived from init's own source there — crossed with three shapes of hand-edited graph file, so 24 runs. MEASURED, and this is item 2 answered with what is true rather than with what it predicted:
 * every branch that CAN meet a file it did not write (--bootstrap, --import, wizard x bootstrap/import/both) tracebacks on all three shapes, and never in one of the four readers this epic consolidated;
 * the shape axis found a THIRD one .24 did not name: 'added: 2026-09-02' loads as a datetime.date, is yielded happily by each_graph_file, and kills init in graph/loader.py::load_graph with TypeError — a different reader from the other two;
 * the residue is exactly two frames over 18 dying runs, read_declared_docs and load_graph, both outside onboarding: one planning decision (beadloom-l22o / BDL-UX #220), not four;
 * --yes (all three modes) is green here for a reason that is NOT a guard working: non_interactive_init returns 'skipped', so the file is never read. The case asserts the message verbatim AND that the file's bytes are untouched, so 'it declined to look' cannot be misread as 'it read it and survived'.
These cases pin a residue rather than cover a fix, so they cannot fail against a tree without a fix. They fail the day beadloom-l22o closes, which is why they exist.

CONTEXT CHANGE FROM beadloom-mr2l.64 (2026-08-24) — this bead stays OPEN and its defect is unchanged. Two things about it moved.

1. SCOPE ITEM 1 IS UNREACHABLE. "Wait for (or read) the first `tests-windows` run" cannot be done: the owner withdrew the leg on cost (~16-28 runner-minutes per PR and the pipeline's critical path, ~3x PR-to-merge latency, for a platform outside the audience). Nothing will adjudicate a prediction on a runner.

2. THE PREDICTION NO LONGER NEEDS ONE. The strict xfail on `test_the_guard_is_told_the_file_the_writer_will_touch` was removed and REPLACED BY A MEASUREMENT, not dropped — a strict xfail no runner can flip is a pin nobody can close. The Windows verdict is now composed from facts asserted on every run:
   - `ntpath.join("src","app.py") == "src\\app.py"` and `PureWindowsPath` reads it as ("src","app.py") — the spelling a Windows harness produces names a real file there;
   - `resolve_edit_path` on that string returns `MALFORMED` + `BACKSLASH_REJECTION` + `relative is None` (the whole verdict, not just the reason);
   - `paths.py` contains no `sys.platform` and no `os.name` — asserted, so the refusal is ONE code path on every OS;
   - `os.fsencode` (the only line in `rejection_reason` whose codec the platform owns) is monkeypatched to raise if reached, and the verdict still comes back — so it cannot depend on it.
   Together: on Windows every edit target is MALFORMED and every guarded edit an `error` at exit 2. That is now measured, not predicted. The review's own argument for the withdrawal is the same one: this bead's false justification is decidable by READING, so buying a Windows leg to adjudicate it was never the cheap way to learn it.

3. SCOPE ITEM 4 IS DONE (doc-only, in .64). `flow-guards/SPEC.md` no longer blames "this build of Beadloom" for the drive-letter behaviour — it says the platform, and states that the documented answer is not the answer on Windows. A new `#### Windows: unverified by decision` section states the consequence of THIS defect without softening, and names the residue nobody will look at.

WHAT REMAINS FOR THIS BEAD, unchanged: items 2, 3 and 5 — the rule (`os.sep`/`os.altsep`), what the shape gate then owes on Windows (reserved device names, trailing dot/space — a stronger argument for a refusal than the backslash ever was), and the requirement that every new rule be exercised where `PureWindowsPath` can settle it. Item 5's second half ("and on the leg where it cannot") now has no leg: whatever cannot be settled by proxy has to be written down as a residual instead, in the section named above. NOTE FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS: the suite now FORBIDS re-pinning a platform prediction on a mark while no runner can adjudicate it (`test_no_platform_xfail_waits_for_a_runner_that_will_not_come`).
